Glad the tests are passing, but there’s an important catch: Python evaluates default arguments once, when the function is defined—not on every call. A list default is shared between calls, so it can leak state rather than reset it.

To get a fresh list each time, use:

def example(items=None):
    if items is None:
        items = []

Can you share the diff? I can help write a PR description that accurately explains the fix—and check whether the passing tests are masking shared state.
